<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.11.1: Graphing Linear Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01046</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.11.1: Graphing Linear Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d5869c8e-d8f7-42ea-8f3d-f27a0b205f25</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the following lines given in slope-intercept form.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00002">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
      <media alt="insert alt text"><iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00003">
                      <media alt="A graph with x and y axes shows two lines: a solid blue line and a dashed red line with a similar slopes.">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_02_01.png"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00004">What is the relationship between the two lines you graphed in question 1?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">They are parallel lines.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00007">When you examine the equations of the lines, what do you notice is the same about the equations?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item>The slopes of the lines are equal.</item>
            <item>The numbers in front of the variables are the same.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00009">When you examine the equations of the lines, what do you notice is different about the equations?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list id="list-00004">
            <item>The constant term is different in each equation.</item>
            <item>The number that is added to the equation is different.</item>
            <item>The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts for each equation is different.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->

        <exercise id="bfcc2540-a04e-4cd5-9e68-013f6be9f291" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            
            <para id="para-00011">When you examine the graph of the lines, how many vertical spaces are between the two lines?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct! There are 6 vertical spaces between the two lines.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00005">
        <item>Use the graphing tool above or technology outside the course, on your coordinate grid, use the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of each equation to graph each equation. Use a different color for each equation.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00006">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
      <media alt="insert alt text"><iframe src="../../media/desmos/ec12a152b538979e2cb489ea202284283765051a.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00014">Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">
                      <media alt="A graph with a solid blue line slanting upward from left to right and a dashed red line slanting downward, both crossing each other near the origin.">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_02_02.png"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00017">When you examine the graph of the lines in question 6, explain if the lines appear to be perpendicular.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <list id="list-00007">
            <item>No, the lines don't look like they intersect at right angles - so they don't look like they are perpendicular. (Check the settings of your graph, this is incorrect.)</item>
            <item>Yes, the lines look like they intersect at right angles - so they appear perpendicular. (This is the correct answer.)</item>
          </list>
          <para id="para-00019">
      Sometimes lines that are perpendicular do not appear to intersect at right angles because of the settings of the graph. To more easily identify perpendicular lines, change the settings of your Desmos graph. 
      In your graph, select the “wrench tool” icon.
      In the grid section of the menu that pops up, select the “Zoom Square” option.
  </para>
          <para id="para-00020">Remember to identify perpendicular lines algebraically, examine their slopes. If the slope values are opposite reciprocals, then the lines intersect at right angles and are perpendicular.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
